Here's a PHP function that generates a unique password with at least 8 characters, consisting of one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, one number, and one special character, using only basic PHP operations such as loops and conditionals. It also takes into account any restrictions or requirements that the user may have for their password, such as a ban on commonly used words or phrases or a prohibition on repeating characters.
```php
function generatePassword($length = 8, $restrictions = array()) {
  $password = '';
  $chars = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789!@#$%^&*()_+-=[]{}|;:,.<>?';
  // Check for banned words or phrases
  foreach ($restrictions as $restriction) {
    if (strpos($password, $restriction) !== false) {
      return generatePassword($length, $restrictions);
    }
  }
  // Generate password
  while (strlen($password) < $length) {
    $char = substr($chars, mt_rand(0, strlen($chars) - 1), 1);
    // Check for repeating characters
    if (strpos($password, $char) !== false) {
      continue;
    }
    // Check for required character types
    if (empty($restrictions) || (in_array('lowercase', $restrictions) && ctype_lower($char)) || (in_array('uppercase', $restrictions) && ctype_upper($char)) || (in_array('number', $restrictions) && is_numeric($char)) || (in_array('special', $restrictions) && !ctype_alnum($char))) {
      $password .= $char;
    }
  }
  return $password;
}
```
To integrate this function into modern web applications, you could use it to generate unique temporary passwords for new users during the registration process. You could also use it to generate secure passphrases for online banking transactions or for any other situation where a strong and unique password is required. Additionally, you could allow users to specify their own password restrictions or requirements, such as a minimum length or a ban on certain characters.

