Sure, here's a Java program that converts decimal numbers to binary using bitwise operators:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class DecimalToBinary {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a decimal number: ");
        int decimal = sc.nextInt();
        int binary[] = new int[32];
        int index = 0;
        while (decimal > 0) {
            binary[index++] = decimal & 1;
            decimal = decimal >> 1;
        }
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = index - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            sb.append(binary[i]).append(" ");
        }
        System.out.println("Binary representation: " + sb.toString());
    }
}
```
The program first prompts the user to enter a decimal number. It then creates an array to hold the binary digits and uses bitwise operators to convert the decimal number to binary. The program then constructs a string with each binary digit separated by a space and prints it to the console.
