Can you provide an example of abstraction and encapsulation in a C++ code?
Abstraction and encapsulation are two important concepts in C++ programming. Abstraction refers to the process of hiding the complexity of a system and exposing only the necessary details to the user. Encapsulation refers to the technique of wrapping data and code together in a single unit.
For example, consider the following C++ code:
class Rectangle {
  private:
    int length;
    int width;
  public:
    void setLength(int l) {
      length = l;
    }
    void setWidth(int w) {
      width = w;
    }
    int getArea() {
      return length * width;
    }
};
In this code, the class Rectangle encapsulates the data and functions related to a rectangle object. The private members length and width are only accessible within the class and are hidden from the user. The public member functions setLength, setWidth, and getArea provide a simplified interface for the user to interact with the rectangle object.
Abstraction can be seen in this code by the fact that the user only needs to interact with the public interface of the Rectangle class and doesn't need to know about the private data members or how they are used to calculate the area.
Encapsulation can be seen in this code by the fact that the data members and functions related to the rectangle object are encapsulated within the class, making it easier to manage and maintain the code.

