Here's a C# code that finds all possible phrases that can be created using the letters in the phrase "invite someone for dinner" and stores them in an array:
```
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string phrase = "invite someone for dinner";
        List<string> phrases = new List<string>();
        for (int i = 0; i < phrase.Length; i++)
        {
            for (int j = 3; j <= phrase.Length - i; j++)
            {
                string substring = phrase.Substring(i, j);
                char[] chars = substring.ToCharArray();
                Array.Sort(chars);
                string sorted = new string(chars);
                if (!phrases.Contains(sorted))
                {
                    phrases.Add(sorted);
                }
            }
        }
        Console.WriteLine("Possible phrases:");
        foreach (string s in phrases.OrderBy(x => x))
        {
            Console.WriteLine(s);
        }
    }
}
```
This code loops through each character in the phrase and generates all possible substrings of length 3 or greater. It then sorts the characters in each substring and checks if the sorted version has already been added to the list of phrases. If it hasn't, it adds the sorted version to the list.
Finally, the code sorts the list of phrases in alphabetical order and prints them to the console.

