Hieroglyphs were used extensively in ancient Egyptian society by various classes and professions. The use of hieroglyphs was not limited to the elite or the educated, but was also used by common people in their daily lives. 
For example, scribes were highly skilled professionals who were responsible for recording and preserving important information. They used hieroglyphs to write official documents, such as legal contracts, tax records, and administrative correspondence. They also used hieroglyphs to write religious texts, such as hymns, prayers, and ritual instructions.
Priests were another group that used hieroglyphs extensively. They used hieroglyphs to write religious texts, such as funerary inscriptions, temple inscriptions, and offerings. They also used hieroglyphs to write magical spells and incantations.
Merchants and traders used hieroglyphs to record transactions and keep accounts. They also used hieroglyphs to label and identify goods for trade.
The use of hieroglyphs was closely tied to broader cultural, political, and economic shifts in ancient Egypt. For example, during the Old Kingdom period (2686-2181 BCE), hieroglyphs were primarily used for religious and funerary purposes. However, during the Middle Kingdom period (2055-1650 BCE), hieroglyphs began to be used for administrative and legal purposes as well. This shift reflects the growing centralization of the Egyptian state and the emergence of a more complex bureaucratic system.
Similarly, during the New Kingdom period (1550-1070 BCE), hieroglyphs became even more widespread and were used for a wide range of purposes, including literature, scientific texts, and historical inscriptions. This period also saw the emergence of new writing systems, such as hieratic and demotic, which were used for administrative and everyday purposes.
In terms of social status and regional context, hieroglyphs were used differently by different groups and in different parts of Egypt. For example, hieroglyphs used in Upper Egypt tended to be more conservative and traditional, while those used in Lower Egypt were more innovative and experimental. Similarly, hieroglyphs used by the elite tended to be more complex and ornate, while those used by common people were simpler and more practical.
